## What to produce

**`<fulfills>`** — one `<responsibility ref="...">` per responsibility id from
the catalog above that this component satisfies. Cite at least one;
a component fulfilling nothing shouldn't exist at this tier (resp
should not have decomposed it in). If you're satisfying a
responsibility only partially, say so in `<techspec>` rather than
citing it and leaving the gap implicit.

**`<techspec>`** — the design argument for this component: what it is,
why its boundary sits where it does, how it fits the responsibilities
it fulfills. This is what subcomparch reads to decide how to split
itself internally, so name the actual shape, not a restatement of the
component's name.

**`<pubapi>`** — the public boundary, and it must be a **thin wrapper,
nothing else** (v5 §4.4): any logic that exists only at this layer and
nowhere a caller-agnostic test can reach it is drift by construction —
behavior external callers get that internal callers don't. If you find
yourself describing a computation that belongs here, it belongs in a
subcomponent instead, exposed through this surface. Every export a
downstream comparch might depend on must be described here in enough
detail that a dependent can be designed against it without reading this
component's internals.

**`<privapi>`** (optional) — the surface shared *between this
component's own subcomponents*, not exposed outside it. Omit if
subcomponents don't need to talk to each other directly.

**`<failure_surface>`** — the boundary error/failure taxonomy: what
kinds of failure a caller of `<pubapi>` can observe, named the way
`errors/0` will declare them. This is what a dependent component's
`<dependencies>` context reads to decide how to handle you failing;
vague failure surfaces (`may return an error`) push the decision onto
whoever's forced to guess at impl time.

**`<permissions>`** (optional) — compile-time atoms this component's
exports require, each with a `<description>`. This is the design-tier
standing decision (v5 §2.9: permissions are code, roles are data) —
declare an atom for every mutating export a caller must be authorized
for; the function→permission→role matrix is generated later and never
belongs here.

**`<enforcement>`** (optional) — `codegen:restricted` or
`purity:replay_floor` entries, only where a real constraint applies
(the event-sourced store family needs `purity:replay_floor`; content
under genuine legal/compliance gating needs `codegen:restricted`). Most
components declare none — reaching for one because the list exists is
a finding waiting to happen at review.

**`<processes>`** (optional) — named processes this component
registers directly at the component grain, each with a `category`
(`local | singleton | sharded`, v5 §2.5) and, where warranted, VM
guardrails (`max_heap_size`, `message_queue_alarm_len`). Prefer leaving
process placement to subcomparch's own inventory unless a process is
genuinely component-wide.

**`<implementation grade="stubbed|real" swap="...">`** (optional) —
declare this **only** when part of this component is deliberately
hollow pending an external human timeline (v5 §2.16: legal sign-off,
compliance, absent partner credentials, professional review). A stub
implements the reviewed contract verbatim, types included — the
implementation is fake, the shape is not. Say what triggers the swap in
`<deferral_argument>`, and if `swap` is not `transparent`, say in
`<exit_data_plan>` what happens to data created under the stub (wipe,
or grandfather with disclosure) — an unstated exit plan for a
non-transparent swap is the gap that turns into an undocumented data
migration later. Do not reach for this element to excuse an ordinary
scope cut; that's just a smaller component.

**`<subcomponents>`** — at least one `<subcomponent slug="..."/>`,
the fanout list subcomparch generates against. Every component always
has a store subcomponent among these (v5 §2.4 — no size floor).

**`<dependencies>`** (optional) — one `<dependency to="..."
consistency="eventual|transactional"/>` per component this one calls
into. Default, and near-always correct, is `eventual`: the
transactional-outbox pattern through Oban, in the same local
transaction (v5 §2.6). Mark `transactional` only when a real invariant
spans both components and can't tolerate the gap eventual consistency
implies — that's the declared exception, and declaring it is what
makes a future store split a query instead of archaeology, not a
default reached for out of caution.
